Go.

I agree with the latest direction overall.

The fixed example-bank approach resolves my concern much better than either pure open-form questions or selectable options.

I particularly agree with:

- no adoption on silence
- skipped fields remaining open rather than populated
- differentiated uncertainty rendering
- the bounded open: line
- fixed example banks rather than dynamically generated ones
- examples being intended to communicate answer shape rather than answer content

One clarification I want to make before consolidation.

My concern was never that users need options.

My concern was that users often do not yet know what kind of answer is being requested.

I think the example-bank solution addresses that.

However, I would explicitly optimize the banks for helping the user understand the dimension, not for helping the user choose an answer.

For example:

Who is this for?

(Describe the intended audience.)

Examples of the kind of answer:
businesses · internal employees · retail investors · students

Describe another audience in your own words, or say "skip".

This feels correct.

The examples teach the shape of a valid response without creating a menu that the user is expected to choose from.

I also agree with your point that the distinction is carried by the structure rather than by a disclaimer.

If the examples are:
- sufficiently general
- spanning
- partially out-of-domain
- fixed rather than generated

then they function as scaffolding rather than recommendations.

On the uncertainty rendering:

I support replacing the previous [UNSPECIFIED] approach with:

- none stated
- not established
- [N/A] — reason
- open: ...

This feels much closer to the original spirit of Goal.

My concern was never preserving uncertainty.

My concern was making every uncertainty look equally important and potentially drawing disproportionate downstream attention to whatever happened to receive a token.

The differentiated rendering plus bounded open: line solves that more cleanly.

One additional note:

The reviewer for this engine should assume no knowledge of my other engines or prompt ecosystem beyond what has been established inside this Goal review itself.

I will be reviewing the other engines separately in fresh sessions, so any rationale that depends on knowledge of those reviews should be restated locally if needed.

Please proceed with:

- integrating the latest changes
- drafting the fixed example banks for the definition slots
- rerunning the worked example with the new uncertainty rendering and question scaffolding

At this point I think the remaining work is primarily calibration and UX, not architecture.
